I was trying to build a short peptide  from scratch in Pinal the other day and  it took me forever to figure out how to  add this carboxyl group right here on  this end the c-terminus right here so I  wanted to share with you what I've  learned since I had trouble finding this  on the pi mol wiki maybe you want to  model a particular amino acid sequence  for yourself or perhaps you're an  instructor creating practice problems  involving amino acid identification this  tutorial will help you build the  sequence you want in Pinal let's begin  feel free to open a PI mol window and  work along with me first let's change  our background to white so it looks good  in a journal or a Word document click  display background and set to white now  click anywhere on the background to get  ready to build in pi mol you can  generate amino acid sequences using the  Builder start with the drop down build  menu to select the secondary structural  element you want to create here under  residue we can select from these three  and let's start with a helix now you can  click alt on your keyboard or the option  key if you're using a Mac plus the one  letter code of the amino acid you'd like  to add to your helix so I'm on a Mac so  option L and we have leucine right here  so we can quickly add amino acids to our  chain  or we can add them manually using the  build drop-down menu build residue  glutamate now I'm using option click to  move this over and I can pinch my  fingers to zoom out a little bit now if  you're using the key command don't stare  at the keyboard while you do this I'm  using version 2.3.4 of Pi Mille and a  few of the amino acids don't work for  example I'm pressing option now and E  and nothing is happening no glutamic  acid so I'll have to go to the build  menu and add this one manually so as a  side note the ones that aren't working  for me with the key command RR e for  glutamic acid I isoleucine and n  asparagine to double check your sequence  go to the display menu and turn your  sequence on we can go to the build menu  switch over to an anti parallel beta  sheet and add more residues to our chain  move my structure over and reorient it a  little bit so you can see so as you  build it'll continue to build along your  sequence here although I'm not quite  sure how to make a turn or anything like  that yet I think you might be able to  place two objects and then connect them  but that's beyond the scope of this  tutorial anyway  so notice on our helix we started by  building from the I got some clipping  going on  we started by building from the  n-terminus over here and that's okay  that looks fine this nitrogen here could  correspond to our NH 3 plus and all is  right in the world  but over on this side on the c-terminus  this is waiting for the next amino acid  so it's just a carbonyl group instead of  a carboxylate and notice this structure  overall looks a little funny we have  this kind of ribbon going along through  our chain and then we also have sticks  this is actually trying to overlay a  cartoon plus sticks so we could just go  to the show menu show as sticks and get  rid of that distracting a little line  there but for now let's delete  everything using the action menu to  right sequence problems for students I  like to use the anti parallel beta sheet  because it's easy to orient the amino  acids in a way that we can see the side  chains clearly so let's set up our new  sequence go to build residue make sure  we're an anti parallel beta sheet and  I'm going to go ahead and use some that  actually work with the key command so  again I'm going to press option or alt  and then we'll do lysine leucine serine  methionine aspartate alanine okay and  for some reason now it's showing up as  lines so let's go back to our show menu  show as sticks since this will be the  view that we want to use now often the  hydrogen atoms are not shown in  renderings like this but building from  scratch  puts the hydrogen atoms on so let's give  the more typical view of this by hiding  the hydrogen atoms this is a typed  command hide hydro  now let me reorient this a little bit  I'm just using my keypad I don't have a  mouse and I'm just clicking on my Mac  option and click and it allows me to  move my structure pinching the fingers  in and out allows me to zoom now our  goal is to add a c-terminus here so  people don't get confused that this is  just ending on a carbon meal so I'll  open the Builder now from this menu  now if this little white rap like  selection thing do you see that right  there if that's missing you may need to  go into the mouse menu and change into  an editing mode so just using my  trackpad the way I'm doing I can use  this two button editing mode and then if  I click anywhere else that'll go away  and if I want to add to this piece right  here I can click and that lets it  highlight up again so I could actually  add on to any other part of this but we  just want to add right here for now so  now with this carbon of the carbonyl  selected I can go in my builder and  click on the CH 4 which will add a  methyl group of course we actually need  this to be oxygen to make it a  carboxylate so click anywhere in the  background but not on the structure that  makes the little white selection tool go  away now click on oxygen in the Builder  it asks which atom we want to replace  and we click on our carbon tada  carboxy terminus built I'll apply the  hide hydro command because notice this  added a little which on the end here and  that's not typically shown so if I click  in the command line I can use the arrow  keys on my computer up and down to  toggle through any of the previous  commands that I use so I can just resis  without typing apply it and we lost our  little H on there we'll close the  builder this is still asking me atoms I  want to replace with oxygen so I'll just  click done over here and now we want to  reorient this so that it's easy to see  the side chains all right  so I'm just clicking and dragging on the  white to move my structure without  selecting anything and we've got this  one here that we really want to show the  branching on okay so I think that's  showing this one pretty well we could  get the gist of our sequence now so  here's our n-terminus c-terminus we can  see all these side chains but this  serine is pretty hard to see  we need to reorient that if we want  somebody to identify this sequence we'll  do this using the mutagenesis wizard so  up in this menu wizard mutagenesis and  select protein if that's one of the  options for you and now it's going to  ask you to pick a residue so this is the  one I want to change so mutagenesis  actually allows you to just move into  different rotamers or you can change the  residue so we're applying no mutation  here but just using these our left and  right arrow keys to move this around so  with the serine residue and that  orientation I think that somebody could  identify all of these amino acid chains  so I'll click apply and my structure has  been reoriented now if we want to  recolor the backbone we'll go up to this  color menu color by element to maintain  our CPK coloring and maybe I'll choose  this salmon color right here and to me  it's really important to make sure that  my image is accessible to colorblind  individuals so I'm going to check this  in a colorblindness simulator I need to  save this as an image file on this is  telling me still to pick a residue so  I'm just gonna click done get that  dismissed from here now I can save this  as a PNG from the file menu I can use  this draw array button if I have it and  this will allow me to save an image to  file or copy it to a clipboard to put it  into a Word document but I'm just gonna  use the command on my Mac to do a good  old-fashioned screen shot and take a  screenshot of this image so now I'll  visit my colorblindness simulator  website Pro anomaly is the most  common form of colorblindness so this is  a